Fixes to the C, C++ and Objective-C tokenizer and preprocessing of lizard
1.26.1. C#, Java, Dart and the other readers built on CLikeReader keep the
behavior they had.

- Raw strings with a custom delimiter, R"x(...)x": read as ordinary strings,
  the quotes, braces and keywords inside them became code.
- Digit separators, 1'000 and 0xFF'FF: the `'` opened a character literal
  that swallowed the code up to the next `'`.
- A character literal ends on its own line, so the lone `'` of
  `#error Don't` pairs with nothing.
- `and` and `or` count as branches in C++ files only; in C and ObjC they are
  plain names.
- `#if`, `#ifdef` and `#elif` add no branch to a function: conditional
  compilation picks one side. Both sides of `#if A / int f() { / #else /
  int f(long) { / #endif` are read, so the `{` the first side left open is
  dropped from the later sides, and the rest of the file is no longer one
  function.
- The mnemonics of an `asm void f() { ... }` or `asm { ... }` body, such as
  `and` and `or`, are no C++ operators.
- `auto&&`, `const&&`, `static_cast<T&&>`, `S&&)` and `S&&,` are rvalue
  references, not a logical `&&`.
- A template header with a parenthesized comparison, `template <bool B =
  (1 < 2)>`, closes at its own `>`, and a header ends at `{`, `}` or `;`.
- A function-try-block, `int f() try { } catch (...) { }`, is one function
  that ends after its last handler.

